    "body": "`BanMan::Ban` computes the ban end as `GetTime() + bantime` (banman.cpp:140). `setban` and `-bantime` both accept any int64, so a relative bantime close to `INT64_MAX`:\n\n```\nsetban 1.2.3.5 add 9223372036854775807\n```\n\nreturns success, but the sum overflows. UBSan reports:\n\n```\nsrc/banman.cpp:140:73: runtime error: signed integer overflow: 1790412558 + 9223372036854775807 cannot be represented in type 'int64_t'\n```\n\nWithout UBSan the result wraps negative, the ban is treated as already expired, and it is silently dropped. With `-bantime=9223372036854775807` the same happens to every `setban add` that uses the default duration.\n\nThis limits relative ban times to uint32 (about 136 years):\n\n- `setban` rejects a relative bantime above `UINT32_MAX` with \"Error: bantime is too large\"\n- `-bantime` is parsed as `uint32_t`, so larger values saturate at `UINT32_MAX`\n\nAbsolute timestamps are unchanged.\n\nThe added cases in `rpc_setban.py` check that `UINT32_MAX + 1` and `INT64_MAX` are rejected, that `UINT32_MAX` is accepted, and that with `-bantime=INT64_MAX` a default-duration ban is listed with a duration of `UINT32_MAX`. Both parts fail on master and pass with the change. `p2p_disconnect_ban.py` still passes.\n\nAI tools were used to help find this issue and to prepare the patch and test.\n",
